<Base lang="en" title="What to Do If Your Certificate Does Not Arrive on Time? UP Janhit Guarantee Act 2011 — the Full Appeal Process" description="Under the UP Janhit Guarantee Act 2011, if your certificate is delayed, the first appeal goes to the SDM and the second to the DM — the 30- and 60-day limits, the penalty, revision and the full process." path="/en/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate/" altPath="/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate/" faqs={faqs} updated="6 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-06" dateModified="2026-11-06">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">What to do if your certificate does not arrive on time?</h1>
<p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in). This article is for information only, not legal advice.</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate-featured.webp" alt="Appeal route under the UP Janhit Guarantee Act, 2011 (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration • Illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<p>You applied for an income, caste or domicile certificate, but weeks have passed and the status is exactly the same? In UP, the answer is not just “wait” — under the <strong>UP Janhit Guarantee Act, 2011</strong>, you have the right to receive the service within a fixed time, and there is also a route to appeal against a delay.</p>
<section class="box"><h2>What is the Act?</h2><p>The Uttar Pradesh Janhit Guarantee Adhiniyam, 2011 — in force from 14 January 2011 — says that government services must be delivered within a fixed time limit. If an application is rejected or the service is not provided within the time limit, you can appeal. Section 6 of the Act sets out the appeal process, Section 7 the penalty rule and Section 8 the provision for revision. The law was made so that ordinary people do not have to keep visiting offices for their work and every service has a fixed limit.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Main parts of the Act</h2><ul><li><strong>Section 3 — Services are notified:</strong> The government decides by notification which service will be delivered in how many days, by which officer (the designated officer) — and who the first appeal officer and the second appellate authority will be.</li><li><strong>Section 4 — Right to a service on time:</strong> Every citizen has the right to receive a notified service within the fixed time.</li><li><strong>Section 5 — Delivering on time:</strong> The designated officer must provide the service within the fixed time.</li><li><strong>Section 6 — Appeal:</strong> On rejection or on expiry of the time limit, the first appeal is within 30 days, and the second appeal on its decision is within 60 days.</li><li><strong>Section 7 — Penalty:</strong> If the first appeal officer does not decide the appeal in time, a penalty of ₹500–₹5,000 applies.</li><li><strong>Section 8 — Revision:</strong> An officer aggrieved by a penalty order can approach the nominated officer for revision within 60 days.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The appeal route — the caste certificate example</h2><p>The Allahabad High Court’s judgments of 2023–2024 have confirmed this chain for caste certificates:</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Where</th><th>By when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application</td><td>Tehsildar (first officer)</td><td>Decision in ~20 days</td></tr><tr><td>First appeal</td><td>SDM</td><td>Within 30 days of rejection or expiry of the time limit; decision in 30 days</td></tr><tr><td>Second appeal</td><td>DM (District Magistrate)</td><td>Within 60 days of the first appeal’s decision</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">Every service has a different time limit — verify your service’s limit from its service-specific notification.</div></section>
<figure class="shot"><img src="/images/blog/janhit-appeal-flow.svg" alt="Janhit Guarantee appeal chain: Tehsildar to SDM to District Magistrate" /><figcaption>Appeal chain under the UP Janhit Guarantee Act, 2011 — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>An example — the full timeline</h2><p>Take an example to understand it — for a caste certificate, going by the 20 days stated by the court:</p><ol><li><strong>1 November:</strong> You applied to the Tehsildar for a caste certificate.</li><li><strong>By 21 November:</strong> A decision should have come — but no answer arrived.</li><li><strong>By 21 December:</strong> You can file the first appeal before the SDM — within 30 days of the time limit expiring.</li><li><strong>The SDM’s decision:</strong> The first appeal should be decided within 30 days.</li><li><strong>If the SDM also does not decide, or rejects the appeal:</strong> You can file the second appeal before the DM within 60 days of their decision.</li></ol><p>This example is only to explain — the actual dates will depend on your application’s submission date and the service’s notified time limit.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>What to write in an appeal</h2><p>When writing an appeal, state these things clearly:</p><ul class="checklist"><li>The name of the service — which certificate you applied for.</li><li>The application / SRN number and the submission date.</li><li>The reason for rejection (if it was rejected) or when the time limit expired.</li><li>What you want — an order to issue the certificate.</li><li>Supporting documents — the receipt, a screenshot of the status, the rejection letter (if any).</li><li>Your name, address and mobile number — so that news of the decision can reach you.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>There is a penalty too</h2><p>Under Section 7 of the Act, if the first appeal officer does not decide the appeal within the fixed time without sufficient cause, the second appellate authority can impose a penalty of <strong>₹500 to ₹5,000</strong> on them. This rule exists so that even an appeal is not left hanging. And under Section 8, a penalty order can also be revised — the aggrieved officer can approach the nominated officer within 60 days.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Appeal vs complaint — understand the difference</h2><p>These are two different routes, and each has its own purpose:</p><ul><li><strong>Complaint (grievance):</strong> Filing a complaint on the IGRS portal or CM Helpline <strong>1076</strong> — this raises the matter and asks for an answer, but it is not a legal appeal.</li><li><strong>Appeal:</strong> The legal route under the Janhit Guarantee Act — here the officer has to decide, and a penalty can also apply if they do not.</li></ul><p>Usually, try a complaint first — the matter is often resolved there. If it is not resolved and the time limit has passed, the appeal route is open. Filing a complaint does not end your right to appeal, but mind the appeal dates — the 30- and 60-day limits run from the submission or decision date.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Before you appeal</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Keep the application / SRN number, receipt and submission date safe.</li><li><a href="/en/status-verification/">Check the status</a> and read the remark — the reason for rejection is written there.</li><li>File a complaint on the helpdesk <strong>0522-2304706</strong> or CM Helpline <strong>1076</strong> and take the complaint number.</li><li>Save the rejection letter or a screenshot of the status — it will help in the appeal.</li><li>If you have documents with a deadline (an admission or recruitment notice), attach them.</li></ul><div class="warn">Pay special attention to the time limits — the first appeal must be filed within 30 days and the second within 60 days. If you are late, you will have to show sufficient cause, and the officer may or may not accept it.</div></section>
